L001134	In both approaches, we use the same bound for the information leakage during the information reconciliation.
L001135	Specifically, we follow~\cite{TSB+22} and use a bound from~\cite{Renes_2012} (combined with~\cite[Corollary~4.10]{DFR20}):
L001136	\begin{equation}\label{eq:leakEC}
L001137	\mathrm{leak}_{\mathrm{EC}}\leqslant n\eta^{\infty}+2\log5\sqrt{n\log\frac{2}{\left(\epsilon_{\mathrm{EC}}^{\mathrm{com}}-
L001138			\tilde{\epsilon}
L001139			\right)^{2}}}+2\log\frac{1}{
L001140		\tilde{\epsilon}
L001141	}+4,
L001142	\end{equation}
L001143	where $\tilde{\epsilon} \in [0,\epsilon_{\mathrm{EC}}^{\mathrm{com}})$ is an arbitrary value that can be optimized over, and $\eta^{\infty}$ is the rate of an optimal EC code in the
L001144	asymptotic limit, i.e.,
L001145	\begin{equation}
L001146	\eta^{\infty}=\left(1-\frac{\gamma_{A}}{2}\right)\left(1-\gamma_{B}\right)h\left(q\right)+\gamma_{A}\gamma_{B}h\left(\omega\right).
L001147	\end{equation}
L001148	Here, $q$ is the honest QBER for $(x,y)=(0,2)$ and $\omega$ is
L001149	the honest average winning probability for $(x,y)\in\{0,1\}^2$.
L001150	
L001151	
L001152	Meanwhile, for the error verification step, we follow~\cite{nadlinger2022experimental} and use an $\epsilon_{\mathrm{EC}}$-almost-universal hash of length 64 bits with $\epsilon_{\mathrm{EC}}=2^{-61}$ (specifically, the VHASH hash family, which is applicable to raw data of size up to $2^{64}$ bits). As discussed in that work, this yields
L001153	\begin{align}
L001154	\mathrm{leak}_{\mathrm{EV}}=64, \qquad
L001155	\epsilon_{\mathrm{cor}} = \epsilon_{\mathrm{EC}} = 2^{-61}.
L001156	\end{align}
